## Runtime evidence (added after ClawSweeper review)

Controlled stand: the driver runs the real `createTelegramDraftStream` from `extensions/telegram/src/draft-stream.ts` against a Telegram API stub whose `sendMessage`/`editMessageText` reject with a network error, inducing the `telegram stream preview failed: ...` path. The stream's `warn` callback is wired exactly as `createTelegramDraftController` wires it on each side: `warn: logVerbose` (origin/main) vs the warn-level subsystem logger with lane/chat/thread context (this branch). Default log configuration, no verbose or debug flags.

Before (origin/main wiring) — the induced failure leaves no operator-visible trace:

```
[stand] mode=BEFORE; default log configuration (no verbose/debug flags)
[stand] inducing a draft preview send failure via rejecting sendMessage...
[stand] done: any warn-level line above (or its absence) is the observable result
```

After (this branch) — the same induced failure emits a warn-level line through the real `telegram/draft-stream` subsystem logger:

```
[stand] mode=AFTER; default log configuration (no verbose/debug flags)
[stand] inducing a draft preview send failure via rejecting sendMessage...
[telegram] stream preview failed: network error: connect ETIMEDOUT (induced by stand)
[stand] done: any warn-level line above (or its absence) is the observable result
```

The same event in the file log carries the full metadata (hostname and local paths redacted):

```json
{"subsystem":"telegram/draft-stream","meta":{"lane":"answer","chatId":424242,"threadId":777},"logLevelName":"WARN","time":"2026-07-19T00:36:36.805+00:00","message":"telegram stream preview failed: network error: connect ETIMEDOUT (induced by stand)"}
```

<details>
<summary>Reproduction driver (tsx .proof/driver.mts BEFORE|AFTER)</summary>

```ts
// Controlled stand for PR evidence: drives the REAL telegram draft stream
// (extensions/telegram/src/draft-stream.ts) against a Telegram API stub whose
// sendMessage rejects, inducing the "telegram stream preview failed" path.
// The stream's `warn` callback is wired exactly as createTelegramDraftController
// wires it on each side:
//   BEFORE (origin/main): warn: logVerbose
//   AFTER  (this branch): warn: (m) => draftLogger.warn(m, { lane, chatId, threadId })
// Usage: tsx .proof/driver.mts BEFORE|AFTER
import { createTelegramDraftStream } from "../extensions/telegram/src/draft-stream.ts";
import { createSubsystemLogger, logVerbose } from "openclaw/plugin-sdk/runtime-env";

const mode = process.argv[2];
if (mode !== "BEFORE" && mode !== "AFTER") throw new Error("usage: driver.mts BEFORE|AFTER");

const draftLogger = createSubsystemLogger("telegram/draft-stream");
const laneName = "answer";
const chatId = 424242;
const threadSpec = { scope: "dm", id: 777 } as const;

const warn =
  mode === "AFTER"
    ? (message: string) =>
        draftLogger.warn(message, { lane: laneName, chatId, threadId: threadSpec.id })
    : (message: string) => logVerbose(message);

const api = {
  sendMessage: async () => {
    throw new Error("network error: connect ETIMEDOUT (induced by stand)");
  },
  editMessageText: async () => {
    throw new Error("network error: connect ETIMEDOUT (induced by stand)");
  },
  deleteMessage: async () => ({}),
} as never;

console.log(`[stand] mode=${mode}; default log configuration (no verbose/debug flags)`);
console.log(`[stand] inducing a draft preview send failure via rejecting sendMessage...`);
const stream = createTelegramDraftStream({
  api,
  chatId,
  thread: { ...threadSpec },
  minInitialChars: 1,
  throttleMs: 250,
  warn,
  log: () => {},
});
stream.update("Working on your request — assembling the summary now.");
await stream.flush();
await stream.stop();
console.log(`[stand] done: any warn-level line above (or its absence) is the observable result`);
process.exit(0);
```

</details>

## What Problem This Solves

`isRecordWithoutThrowing`, `readRecordValue`, `copyArrayEntries`, and `copyRecordEntries` are exported defensive guards against hostile Proxy objects with 0% test coverage.

## Evidence

```
$ node --import tsx -e "
import { isRecordWithoutThrowing, readRecordValue, copyArrayEntries } from './src/shared/safe-record.js';
const hostile = new Proxy({}, { get() { throw new Error('boom'); } });
console.log('throwing Proxy:', isRecordWithoutThrowing(hostile), '(typeof/Array.isArray are trap-safe)');
console.log('readRecordValue on Proxy:', readRecordValue(hostile, 'key'), '(get trap caught → undefined)');
console.log('readRecordValue normal:', readRecordValue({name:'test'}, 'name'));
console.log('null copyArrayEntries:', JSON.stringify(copyArrayEntries(null)));
"

throwing Proxy: true (typeof/Array.isArray are trap-safe)
readRecordValue on Proxy: undefined (get trap caught → undefined)
readRecordValue normal: test
null copyArrayEntries: []

$ npx vitest run src/shared/safe-record.test.ts --reporter=verbose
 Test Files  1 passed (1)
      Tests  15 passed (15)
```

## Summary

- add dynamic configured-model choices to the native `/model` command
- enable the existing command argument menu path for `/model` so Slack can render model picks with its existing buttons/static_select/external_select machinery
- keep bare `/model` status behavior when no configured model choices are available

## Details

`/think` already gets rich Slack command UI because its registry entry declares choices plus an argument menu. `/model` had a positional argument but no choices/menu metadata, so Slack had nothing to render before dispatching the command as plain text.

This change keeps the fix in the shared command registry: configured catalog entries become `provider/model` choices, and the existing `/model <provider/model>` setter handles the selected value. No Slack-specific command renderer changes are needed.

## Verification

- `node scripts/run-vitest.mjs src/auto-reply/commands-registry.test.ts`
- `git diff --check HEAD~1..HEAD`
- `node --import tsx - <<'EOF' ...` registry smoke shown below

## Real behavior proof

Behavior addressed: Slack native slash-command UX for `/model` can now surface the existing command argument menu instead of requiring users to type a model id manually.

Real environment tested: local OpenClaw source checkout in an isolated worktree from `origin/main`, running the actual command registry module after this patch.

Exact steps or command run after this patch: ran this local terminal command against the patched checkout:

```bash
node --import tsx - <<'EOF'
import { findCommandByNativeName, resolveCommandArgMenu } from './src/auto-reply/commands-registry.ts';
const command = findCommandByNativeName('model');
if (!command) throw new Error('missing /model command');
const menu = resolveCommandArgMenu({
  command,
  cfg: {
    models: {
      providers: {
        openai: { models: [{ id: 'gpt-5.5', name: 'GPT-5.5' }] },
        anthropic: { models: [{ id: 'claude-sonnet-4.6', name: 'Claude Sonnet 4.6' }] },
      },
    },
  },
});
console.log(JSON.stringify({ arg: menu?.arg.name, choices: menu?.choices }, null, 2));
EOF
```

Evidence after fix: terminal output from the patched OpenClaw registry smoke:

```json
{
  "arg": "model",
  "choices": [
    {
      "value": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-4.6",
      "label": "anthropic/Claude Sonnet 4.6"
    },
    {
      "value": "openai/gpt-5.5",
      "label": "openai/GPT-5.5"
    }
  ]
}
```

Observed result after fix: `/model` now resolves a native command argument menu for configured model choices, using `provider/model` values that the existing `/model <provider/model>` setter dispatch path already accepts. Focused registry tests also passed 31/31.

What was not tested: live Slack Block Kit interaction against a real Slack workspace. The Slack plugin path reused here is already covered by existing arg-menu render/dispatch tests; this PR adds the missing registry metadata that feeds that path.

## Notes

AI-assisted with human-requested agent workflow. The local commit helper hydrated ignored `node_modules` in the isolated worktree before committing; no tracked dependency or lockfile changes are included.

